# Mutation case for the object key order table (ARCHITECTURE.md, "JSON Object Key
# Order") as the C11 backend reads it. Unlike the other tables, C11's writer is held to
# this one by a cargo test that generates a C machine and compiles it, not by a ctest
# unit, so the casefile runs that test.
#
# Run with `scripts/mutate sce-build/tests/mutations/c_reads_the_shared_key_order_table.cases`.
#
# The mutant changes the expected data of one flat case to an order no engine writes and
# leaves the table well formed, so only a test that loads the table can tell it from the
# right one. A SURVIVED here means the C11 key order test carries its own copy of the
# table's expectations, or does not read the table.
#
# The anchor is a literal slice of the table; an edit that moves it is reported
# INCONCLUSIVE with the anchor quoted, never as a pass.

mutation_tests -p sce-build --features cli --test a_c_machines_event_data_follows_the_shared_key_order
mutation_runtime_targets tests/json_text/object_key_order.json
mutation_oracles sce-build/tests/a_c_machines_event_data_follows_the_shared_key_order.rs

mutation_case "an object's key order is written down wrongly" <<'PY'
edit("tests/json_text/object_key_order.json",
     r'''{ "name": "declaration order is not the written order", "params": [["b", 1], ["a", 2]], "data": "{\"a\":2,\"b\":1}" }''',
     r'''{ "name": "declaration order is not the written order", "params": [["b", 1], ["a", 2]], "data": "{\"b\":1,\"a\":2}" }''')
PY
